i'm jane secker and this is the last and  final episode of storycast 21.  when we set out to make this series we  asked our journalists and producers to  share with us the one story they worked  on which has stayed with them more than  any other this century  the responses were many and varied and  we took from these ideas to bring you a  selection of personal stories from just  some of the century's biggest news  events  but 21 years ago as we readied ourselves  for this new century we found ourselves  reporting on what was then expected to  be the biggest story of our times  something not unlike the covert 19  pandemic of today and that this story  had the potential to impact everyone all  across the world  the very first story of 2000 revolved  around what was then a worldwide effort  to combat a global computer bug  episode 21 of storycast 21 tells one  man's story from that crisis  this is the y2k prison bug  [Music]  the so-called year 2000 computer bug has  generated fears that anything from  nuclear missiles to interbank trading  systems could fail when 1999 turns into  the year 2000  my name is rob mulhern and i'm the  series producer of sky news storycast  back in december 1999 people were pretty  nervous about the dawn of a new  millennium  experts had long been predicting that on  the stroke of midnight on the 1st of  january 2000  the computers that managed not only our  economies but our lives  would fail  [Applause]  the problem is systems thinking 0-0 the  shorthand for 2000 is instead 1900.  organizations have spent months and  years aiming to avoid this  no one it seemed taught to program  computers to recognize dates beyond the  31st of december 1999. often they  thought it was 1980 or 1976  and sometimes those systems didn't  handle that change very well at all and  crashed  this is matthew hackling he's a cyber  security consultant from perth  and back in 1999 i myself was living in  australia  among the first major economies  set to experience the millennium day  change  failing to guard against the problem is  threatening to bring the country to a  standstill from telecommunications to  banking  i remember vividly the australian  government taking y2k really seriously  how the collapse of computer systems may  lead get this  to the collapse of civilization itself  and as well as the threats to the  economy there were huge concerns around  law and order  and one area in particular  in an australian prison there's really  not that many prison officers on the  facility they use technologies to detect  people breaking in and people breaking  out and wouldn't really want the doors  on the most secure facility to pop open  on new year's eve  back in 1999  matthew hackling was handed one of the  toughest jobs of the entire y2k crisis  fixing the computer book that was set to  throw into chaos the australian prison  system  thereby handing inmates a possible  escape  the countdown was on  [Music]  you might expect a solution to a future  national computer crisis to begin in an  i.t lab somewhere  but the road to solving this programming  problem actually started in the quiet  suburban bedroom of matthew's home in  perth western australia  i was very much into computers from a  young age  both my parents are academics and so i  grew up on university networks  my mother was a mainframe operator for  the university of western australia  i used to play online games  with uni students when i was you know  knee-high  despite the fact that matthew was still  just a kid in the 1980s  he quickly went from playing games to  making them  when i was a kid one of the first  programs that i programmed was  coded in from a magazine and it was like  a little game where you flew a spaceship  um down like a ravine and didn't crash  into the walls  and um yeah i was pretty impressed with  myself when i got that to work  matthew's love of computers it stayed  with him through his teenage years  by 1997 he did a university degree in  i.t and security management  and by 1999 when computers pretty much  ran everything in the world  matthew had found himself in a  specialist role with the ministry of  justice his job securing australia's  toughest prison  as well as the countless economic issues  thrown up by the millennium bug  and in terms of this story  the government of western australia were  now in a race against time to try and  make their j of y2k compliant  so the worst case scenario for a maximum  security prison would be of course like  somebody breaking in to break out  prisoners  when things went a little bit haywire or  a prisoners organizing a prison break  it was now  nine months to go to the day change  matthew's first major assignment  actually turned out to be the state's  toughest jail  casarina prison  located on the outskirts of perth  and the scene of a christmas day prison  riot only months before  [Music]  the one that always sticks in my mind is  kasharina  prison because uh that's the um with the  premier maximum security prison in  western australia  but uh yeah it is kind of like in a kind  of arid  dusty sandy area  and um it has very high razor wire  fences  there is uh you know  uh you know armed response vehicles that  patrol the perimeter of the prison  and the perimeter of the prison does not  have a break in it the um the gatehouse  is actually underneath the um the razor  wire  and that's designed for you know maximum  security  and i tell you what it's pretty spooky  going into that facility the first time  especially when you do this long long  walk from the gatehouse to the  to the center of the prison  [Music]  we hear is the whistle of the wind  through the reservoir  and is very eerie eerie feeling  because cassarina prison was home to  some of australia's most dangerous  criminals  security depended as much on the 24-hour  surveillance cameras as the prison  guards  in simple terms  this technology was controlled by a  network of clocks and timers  that opened and shut cell doors that  turned on and off cameras  so all those sort of systems be they you  know closed  detection systems or access control  systems intrusion detection  they all work on you know on computer  systems and embedded systems  if it came to pass that these clocks  stopped working when the date changed  from 1999 to 2000  well  this may inadvertently hand thousands of  prisoners the chance of an escape  in any maximum security prison where you  know people are getting locked up for  you know multiple decades for doing  really bad type stuff  yeah they're always looking for a way to  get out so  but if the security problem in katarina  were to be dealt with properly then  matthew and his fellow team of engineers  would themselves need to be locked  inside  we had as built drawings show where all  the  field equipment was throughout the  prison but we really didn't know whether  there were clocks in them or not  only then  could they set off in search of the  clocks and timers that needed to be  fixed so as to prevent against  jailbreaks  when i was in the prison a lot of the  sort of the the technologies they're  hidden away from the inmates um you know  in roof spaces or in equipment cabinets  so there was a lot of sort of you know  getting into places with dusty places  that people hadn't been to for a while  that they didn't have a clock in them  and yeah and so yeah i did you did end  up crawling through a lot of a lot of  roof spaces because that's where we put  a lot of security technologies and so it  was really an activity of you know  writing down serial numbers and model  numbers of devices and performing  inventory  and what we did is if it had a clock in  it then we sort of we tested it  the difficulty is even though all this  work has been done on the millennium bug  it remains in many areas an issue of  public confidence  weeks quickly turned into months and as  matthew said about his work in casarina  and the other maximum security prisons  in western australia  beyond the prison walls  public anxiety around the y2k crisis  continue to ramp up  airlines show 13 at some risk  remarkable as it might sound planes  falling from the sky and bank accounts  being wiped if their balances  were among the chief concerns both  domestically and internationally eight  major financial institutions also face  severe risk but if y2k was starting to  feel like a bad science fiction movie  then matthew who by now believed he  tracked down nearly all the prison  security systems  was finding that work on the ground in  one of the assigned maximum security  prisons was anything but  yeah we  actually  had some issues with a cell intercom  system  and these systems are in cells so that  our prisoners can press a button and  talk to a guard in the gatehouse  when we rolled the system over to 2000  at this prison all the cell intercom  systems went off  the system clocked over to 1980  [Music]  it was for this very situation that the  ministry of justice hired matthew  a situation where prison guards  outnumbered by prisoners  may well find themselves cut off and  unable to communicate  not just with inmates but more  critically each other  a nightmare scenario however remote  already it was apparent to lots of  prisoners and some with no hope of ever  being released that the y2k bug could  well compromise security in their  individual prison and um yeah that was  um  that was unexpected  and uh what we ended up doing is we  ended up upgrading that system  to newer firmware on the field equipment  so that it would work properly after the  ytk turnover  against this backdrop well it wouldn't  be entirely accurate to say the ministry  of justice had complete faith in the  security upgrade work  in a maximum security prison you know  there are people who are armed on-site  the moj did have a lot of sort of  contingency planning and they did sort  of run you know  a crisis management type activity and  you know  i suspect they might have put a few  people like extra people on um for you  know for the ytk shift no one was just  sort of crossing their fingers and you  know hoping that everything would work  out all right  by now spring had given way to summer in  australia and little by little prism  wing by prison wing matthew and his  colleagues had completed the upgrade  work in not only casarina  but western australia's other maximum  security prisons there's also growing  concern  systems will experience computer  breakdowns in the year 2000  by now  dramatic y2k news reports from across  the world were impossible to avoid  across the world billions of pounds been  spent on making computers y2k compliant  perhaps none's more important than the  computers that run the nuclear bunkers  of russia and america  waking up on new year's eve against the  backdrop of reports like that one  citing possible nuclear armageddon  matthew took comfort in the fact that  he'd found every clock he'd fixed every  timer  at least he hoped so i had a lot of a  bit of professional sort of um you know  curiosity is like did we really get  everything well you know well you know  if we didn't get everything it couldn't  be a system that was now critical  [Music]  either way the ministry of justice was  now mere minutes and thousands of alert  prisoners away  from the final countdown of the  millennium  and um my wife was actually drumming in  a samba band  [Music]  i joined her marching through the  streets of perth  to a party on top of a building in the  subiaco  [Music]  julie i know a lot of people were  watching australia the first sign of any  possible trouble so i wasn't too too  concerned but i tell you i did get some  extra cash out of the atm  just in case the atms were shut down for  a couple of days after work occurred  and i made sure i wasn't in the plane  and i made sure i wasn't on the road  [Applause]  [Music]  ultimately though  at around one minute past 12 on new  year's day 2000.  the cell doors inside western  australia's maximum security prisons  remained firmly closed  uh it was it was on my mind until i fir  until we finished and started cracked  open the first beer  but i was happy to  you know not be around a lot of  technology and not being responsible for  a lot of technology  [Music]  we ended up finishing up at a party on  the top of a  parking garage i believe  [Music]  in the minutes and hours that followed  news bulletins carried no reports of  planes falling from the sky  large computer networks continued to  function  and while there were plenty of lost bank  cards  no one lost a bank account or saw their  bank balance wiped  whether this is entirely down to the  estimated 500 billion dollars spent  worldwide on making systems y2k  compliant  is well  something that's still open to debate  i can't recall any really major  incidents  i'm sure if we looked on wikipedia we  might find some  yeah so i look finally back on those  times  and i'm really thankful to the people  that that gave me the opportunity to  work there at the engineering company  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  the y2k prison bug was written and  produced by rob mulhern with recordings  from tom gillespie special thanks to  matthew hackling and all our  contributors over the last 21 episodes  for more information on this episode and  all of the other 20 of course go to sky  news dot com forward slash storycast 21.  finally thanks to everyone who made this  series which was written produced and  sound mixed by rob mulhern research  recordings and digital by tom gillespie  archive with simon windsor the tv  treatments victoria hudson grant and  emily dieker the story consultant dave  terrace the design and graphics brian  gillingham and for social media chris  scott and sam gould  if you enjoyed the series or just one  particular episode please share comment  or rate on the platform of your podcast  provider  storycast will return again in 2022  i'm jane sekker thank you for listening  happy new year australia welcome to the  21st century on this magnificent night  the millennium night eternity over  sydney harbour  [Music]  [Music]  you

